<h1>ISR → Layer 0 + HTTP Cache Semantics</h1>
<p style="color:var(--ui-text-muted);margin-bottom:1rem">Incremental Static Regeneration is a compensation for a compensation for a compensation.</p>

<htx:component src="/ui/alert.htx" variant="info" title="The Compensation Chain">
  SSR compensates for missing representations → SSG compensates for SSR performance → ISR compensates for SSG staleness. Three layers of compensation for a property HTTP provides natively.
</htx:component>

<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:2rem;margin-top:2rem">
  <div>
    <h3>React (Next.js ISR)</h3>
    <pre style="background:#1e1e2e;color:#cdd6f4;padding:1rem;border-radius:8px;font-size:0.8rem;overflow-x:auto;line-height:1.6"><code>export async function getStaticProps() {
  const posts = await fetchPosts();
  return {
    props: { posts },
    revalidate: 60, // seconds
  };
}
// Also needs: on-demand revalidation API,
// cache invalidation strategy,
// reasoning about stale content windows</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h3>PRESTO (standard HTTP)</h3>
    <pre style="background:#1e1e2e;color:#cdd6f4;padding:1rem;border-radius:8px;font-size:0.8rem;overflow-x:auto;line-height:1.6"><code>Cache-Control: public,
  max-age=60,
  stale-while-revalidate=300

// That's it. Standard HTTP.
// Any CDN or reverse proxy handles this.
// The template is unchanged.
// No revalidation API.
// No on-demand cache busting endpoint.
// HTTP has done this since 1999 (RFC 2616).</code></pre>
  </div>
</div>

<p style="margin-top:1.5rem"><a href="/reorientation">&larr; All reorientations</a></p>
